Burbea is the only piece of your stack that actually solves this, which is why you should center him, not Ñāṇananda. Burbea taught orthodox emptiness for years (Seeing That Frees is as rigorous as Ñāṇananda, arguably more systematic) and then built Soulmaking Dharma specifically because he ran into this exact wall — total deconstruction is real and valid, but it leaves no room for image, eros, calling, or the daimonic. His resolution: emptiness doesn't mean images are false, it means images aren't inherently existing — which frees you to relate to a figure (a daimon, a thoughtform, an elemental power) as genuinely real-in-relationship, sacred, worth devotion, without smuggling in a fixed metaphysical self underneath it. Nothing is denied reality, nothing is granted inherent existence. That's your actual engine, not raw dependent origination. Read the Soulmaking Dharma talks directly (they're free, dharma.org has them), specifically the material on "emptiness and desire" and "the sacred and the imaginal" — he addresses your exact question head-on, better than I can restate it.
Where Kastrup actually earns his place: he gives you metaphysical permission to take the daimon seriously as literally other, without needing either (a) naive supernatural realism or (b) reductive "it's just my psychology" flattening. His frame: you're a dissociated alter of one mind, the daimon is what he calls the "meta-self" — a deeper segment of that same mind, on the far side of your dissociative boundary, showing up as an autonomous-feeling presence with knowledge you don't consciously have. That's a real ontological claim, not a metaphor, and it's compatible with Burbea's non-inherent-existence framing because Kastrup's "mind at large" isn't a fixed self either — it's the one thing that's real, and every alter/daimon within it is empty of separate existence exactly the way Burbea needs them to be. This is a cleaner fit than Jung alone, because Jung stayed agnostic about literal ontology and just described the psychological function. You're asking for more than function — you want the daimon to be actually real enough to embody. Kastrup gives you that without violating anatta.
Jung's actual contribution here isn't individuation-as-integration (drop that framing, it doesn't fit your stack) — it's active imagination as method. That's the technique: engage what arises in scrying/thoughtform work as if it has its own will, let it talk back, don't pre-decide what it means. That's practice-level, not theory-level, and it works fine inside a Buddhist-empty/Kastrup-real frame.
Structural proposal, concretely:

Elemental scrying/thoughtform work generates the raw material — same as you're doing now.
When something charged/repetitive shows up (a compulsive pattern, a recurring image, a felt "blockage"), don't reach first for Jungian integration ("what does this mean about my psyche") — reach for dependent origination: what conditions is this arising dependent on? What's it propped up against? See it as a process, not a possession. This is where Ñāṇananda's material actually belongs in your stack — not as the whole engine, but as the specific move you make on compulsive/unconscious material to loosen its grip. It's a scalpel, not the foundation.
Once it's loosened — not owned, not fought, just seen as empty of inherent self — that's exactly the condition Burbea says frees you to engage it as image/daimon without reifying it. You're not identifying with the pattern anymore, so you can now meet it as a figure, give it soul, let it be sacred, without it running you unconsciously the way it was.
Sigils and talismans become the vessel for that daimonic material specifically after step 3, not before. Powering a talisman with unprocessed compulsive material just re-embeds the unconscious pattern in a more powerful container — you'd be arming the exact loop you're trying to see through. Powering it with material that's passed through the emptiness-move is what actually lets you "channel the daimon into embodiment" on purpose, as a chosen conduit, instead of it running you as an unrecognized repetition.
Kastrup underwrites the whole thing by letting you treat the daimon, once it shows up in step 3-4, as literally a real other segment of mind you're in relationship with — not just useful fiction, not literal separate god either.